cayleyTrick -- Cayley trick
***************************

  * Usage: 
        cayleyTrick(I,k)
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a homogeneous ideal defining a
        projective variety $X=V(I)\subset\mathbb{P}^n$
      * k, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the dimension of $X$, or more
        generally an integer $k$ not exceeding the dimension of $X$ and not
        smaller than the dual defect of $X$
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Duality: Duality, => ..., default value false, whether to use
        dual Plücker coordinates
      * *note Variable: Grass_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVariable=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, specify a name for a variable
  * Outputs:
      * a pair $(J,J')$, where $J$ is the defining ideal of the Segre product
        $X\times\mathbb{P}^k\subset\mathbb{P}^{(k+1)(n+1)-1}$, while $J'$ is
        the principal ideal defining the dual hypersurface
        $(X\times\mathbb{P}^k)^{*}\subset{\mathbb{P^{(k+1)(n+1)-1}}}^{*}$

Description
===========

Let $X\subset\mathbb{P}^n$ be a $k$-dimensional projective variety. Consider
the product $W = X\times\mathbb{P}^k$ as a subvariety of
$\mathbb{P}(Mat(k+1,n+1))$, the projectivization of the space of $(k+1)\times
(n+1)$-matrices, and consider the projection
$p:\mathbb{P}(Mat(k+1,n+1))\dashrightarrow\mathbb{G}(k,n)=\mathbb{G}(n-k-1,n)$.
Then the "Cayley trick" states that the dual variety $W^*$ of $W$ equals the
closure of $p^{-1}(Z_0(X))$, where $Z_0(X)\subset\mathbb{G}(n-k-1,n)$ is the
Chow hypersurface of $X$. The defining form of $W^*$ is also called the
$X$-resultant. For details and proof, see Multiplicative properties of
projectively dual varieties (see
http://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF02567693 ), by J. Weyman and A.
Zelevinsky; see also Coisotropic hypersurfaces in Grassmannians (see
https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0747717119301506 ), by
K. Kohn.

In the example below, we apply the method to the quadric
$\mathbb{P}^1\times\mathbb{P}^1\subset\mathbb{P}^3$.

+------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : QQ[x_0..x_3]; P1xP1 = ideal(x_0*x_1-x_2*x_3)     |
|                                                      |
|o2 = ideal(x x  - x x )                               |
|            0 1    2 3                                |
|                                                      |
|o2 : Ideal of QQ[x ..x ]                              |
|                  0   3                               |
+------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : time (P1xP1xP2,P1xP1xP2') = cayleyTrick(P1xP1,2);|
| -- used 0.132821s (cpu); 0.0907819s (thread); 0s (gc)|
+------------------------------------------------------+

