$Y\subset\mathbb{G}(k,\mathbb{P}^n)$, then the method returns the ideal of the
variety $W\subset\mathbb{P}^n$ swept out by the linear spaces corresponding to
points of $Y$. As an example, we now compute a surface scroll
$W\subset\mathbb{P}^4$ over an elliptic curve
$Y\subset\mathbb{G}(1,\mathbb{P}^4)$.

+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : Y = ideal apply(5,i->random(1,G'1'4)); -- an elliptic curve|
|                                                                |
|o7 : Ideal of G'1'4                                             |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : time W = plucker Y; -- surface swept out by the lines of Y |
| -- used 0.107806s (cpu); 0.0624566s (thread); 0s (gc)          |
|                                                                |
|o8 : Ideal of P4                                                |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : (codim W,degree W)                                         |
|                                                                |
|o9 = (2, 5)                                                     |
|                                                                |
|o9 : Sequence                                                   |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+

In this example, we can recover the subvariety
$Y\subset\mathbb{G}(k,\mathbb{P}^n)$ by computing the Fano variety of
$k$-planes contained in $W$.

+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : time Y' = plucker(W,1); -- variety of lines contained in W|
| -- used 0.192291s (cpu); 0.192294s (thread); 0s (gc)           |
|                                                                |
|o10 : Ideal of G'1'4                                            |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : assert(Y' == Y)                                           |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+

Warning: Notice that, by default, the computation is done on a randomly chosen
affine chart on the Grassmannian. To change this behavior, you can use the
*note AffineChartGrass: AffineChartGrass, option.

Ways to use plucker:
====================

  * "plucker(Ideal)"
  * "plucker(Ideal,ZZ)"

