File: Resultants.info, Node: tangentialChowForm, Next: veronese, Prev: SingularLocus, Up: Top

tangentialChowForm -- higher Chow forms of a projective variety
***************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        tangentialChowForm(I,s)
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a homogeneous ideal defining a
        projective variety $X=V(I)\subset\mathbb{P}^n$, say of dimension $k$
      * s, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note AffineChartGrass: AffineChartGrass, => ..., default value true,
        use an affine chart on the Grassmannian
      * *note AffineChartProj: AffineChartProj, => ..., default value true, use
        an affine chart on the projective space
      * *note AssumeOrdinary: AssumeOrdinary, => ..., default value null,
        whether the expected codimension is 1
      * *note Duality: Duality, => ..., default value null, whether to use dual
        Plücker coordinates
      * *note SingularLocus: SingularLocus, => ..., default value null, pass
        the singular locus of the variety
      * *note Variable: Grass_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVariable=>_pd_pd_pd_rp, => ...,
        default value null, specify a name for a variable
  * Outputs:
      * a *note ring element: (Macaulay2Doc)RingElement,, or an *note ideal:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal, (if there is more than one generator) in the
        coordinate ring of the Grassmannian $\mathbb{G}(n-k-1+s,\mathbb{P}^n)$
        in the Plücker embedding, representing the higher associated subvariety
        $Z_s(X)$

Description
===========

For a projective variety $X\subset\mathbb{P}^n$ of dimension $k$, the $s$-th
associated subvariety $Z_s(X)\subset\mathbb{G}(n-k-1+s,\mathbb{P}^n)$ (also
called tangential Chow form) is defined to be the closure of the set of
$(n-k-1+s)$-dimensional subspaces $L\subset \mathbb{P}^n$ such that $L\cap
X\neq\emptyset$ and $dim(L\cap T_x(X))\geq s$ for some smooth point $x\in L\cap
X$, where $T_x(X)$ denotes the embedded tangent space to $X$ at $x$. In
particular, $Z_0(X)\subset\mathbb{G}(n-k-1,\mathbb{P}^n)$ is defined by the
Chow form of $X$, while $Z_k(X)\subset\mathbb{G}(n-1,\mathbb{P}^n)$ is
identified to the dual variety
$X^{*}\subset{\mathbb{P}^n}^{*}=\mathbb{G}(0,{\mathbb{P}^n}^{*})$ via the
duality of Grassmannians $\mathbb{G}(0,{\mathbb{P}^n}^{*})=\mathbb{G}(n-1,\
mathbb{P}^n)$. For details we refer to the third chapter of Discriminants,
Resultants, and Multidimensional Determinants (see
http://link.springer.com/book/10.1007%2F978-0-8176-4771-1 ), by Israel M.
Gelfand, Mikhail M. Kapranov and Andrei V. Zelevinsky.

The algorithm used are standard, based on projections of suitable incidence
varieties. Here are some of the options available that could speed up the
computation.

Duality Taking into account the duality of Grassmannians, one can perform the
computation in $\mathbb{G}(k-s,n)$ and then passing to $\mathbb{G}(n-k-1+s,n)$.
This is done by default when it seems advantageous.

AffineChartGrass If one of the standard coordinate charts on the Grassmannian
is specified, then the internal computation is done on that chart. By default,
a random chart is used. Set this to false to not use any chart.

AffineChartProj This is quite similar to AffineChartGrass, but it allows to
specify one of the standard coordinate charts on the projective space. You
should set this to false for working with reducible or degenerate varieties.

AssumeOrdinary Set this to true if you know that $Z_s(X)$ is a hypersurface (by
default is already true if $s=0$).

